@flexiarg baldwin/ablation-axes-must-not-disable-the-instrument
@title An Ablation Axis Must Not Also Switch Off the Measurement
@sigils [🔦/双]
@keywords ablation, learning-budget, construct-validity, measurement, confound, learning-curve, metaca
@audience experiment preregistrars, MetaCA experiment designers
@tone exacting
@style pattern
@childof baldwin/mechanism
@references [baldwin/test-evolution-of-learnability-before-static-assimilation baldwin/plasticity-builds-a-selective-neighbourhood]

! conclusion:
  When the mechanism under study is also the mechanism the instrument depends on, an
  ablation of that mechanism does not produce a dose-response curve — it produces a
  curve of how much instrument remains. Check that the ablation axis and the
  measurement window are disjoint before reading anything off the curve.

  + context:
    The MetaCA guidance pilot varied a learning budget over `[0, 4, 16, 64, 120]` and
    read a learning curve from it. The budget gates rewriting by absolute timestep,
    while causal reach perturbs at `t* = 60` and measures divergence at `t* + 59`. So
    budgets 0, 4 and 16 switch rewriting off entirely before the perturbation, and
    budget 64 leaves four of fifty-nine live steps. The low-budget points measure the
    static-field condition, not a partially trained one.

  + IF:
    A learning-curve, dose-response, or truncated-budget design ablates the same
    process that the outcome measure reads.

  + HOWEVER:
    The defect is invisible in the summary statistic and survives every apparatus check
    that asks whether arms were separated, tapes aligned, or configurations valid. Those
    checks confirm the axis was applied; none of them asks whether the axis means what
    the registration says it means. Replacing a censored readout with a continuous one
    leaves the confound untouched.

  + THEN:
    Separate the two intervals explicitly. Vary the adaptation budget over a training
    interval that ends before the measurement window opens, and hold the mechanism
    identical across arms inside the measurement window; or adopt a readout that does
    not require the ablated mechanism to be live. Register the intervals, not only the
    budget levels, and state the predicted value of the zero-budget arm in advance.

  + BECAUSE:
    A dose-response reading assumes the treatment changes the system and the instrument
    reports on it. If the treatment changes the instrument, the curve is a mixture whose
    two components cannot be separated after the fact, and the arms are not comparable
    even though they are correctly randomized and correctly separated.

    + EVIDENCE:
      The zero-budget arm reproduced the all-held static reach (`1.18`--`1.41` against
      `1.1778`), which identifies it as the static-field condition rather than an
      untrained learner. Mean reach was also non-monotone — budgets 4 and 16 fell below
      budget 0 in all four population-by-partition cells — as expected when a few steps
      of rewriting scramble the inherited field and then freeze the result. The
      preregistered diagnosis attributed the null to a zero-inflated preparedness
      statistic, which is true but downstream of this.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If the zero-budget arm had been predicted in advance and had matched the static
      control, the identity of the axis would have been caught before the run rather
      than in review.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Add a registration field naming the interval each ablation applies to.
    - Require a predicted value for every ablation endpoint, checked at smoke time.
    - Treat "the curve is flat at low dose" as a construct-validity alarm before it is a power problem.
